ical threat.\n\nLet’s recheck occupied:\n- H7: X (Black)\n- H8: O (White)\n- H9: X (Black)\n\nSo column H: X-O-X — no threat.\n\nBlack has I9 and H9 — so bottom-right from I8? I8 is White.\n\nWait — Black has H7, H9, I9 — is there a diagonal?\n\n- H7 (X), I8 (O), J9 (?) — I8 is White, so no.\n\n- H9 (X), I9 (X) — two in a row horizontally on row 9: H9, I9 — if J9 is played, it becomes three. But not urgent.\n\nWhite’s strongest potential is row 8: G8, H8, I8 all White — that’s three in a row horizontally.\n\nExtending to J8 would make four — very strong.\n\nIs J8 available? Not in occupied list — yes, it\'s free.\n\nIf White plays J8, then G8-H8-I8-J8 — four in a row, open on both ends (F8 and K8). That creates a double threat: next move F8 or K8 both win. But more importantly, **is there a way to make five now?**\n\nNo — no single move makes five.\n\nBut let’s check: is there a **double threat** move?\n\nWhat about F8? Playing F8 would give White F8-G8-H8-I8 — four in a row. Also powerful.\n\nBut J8 extends the right side — same effect.\n\nHowever, **J8 may be better** because:\n- It keeps symmetry and central alignment.\n- Looking ahead, the center is more valuable.\n\nBut wait — what about **I7** or other forks?\n\nCheck diagonal: G8, H7 — H7 is Black, so no.\n\nG8, H8, I8 — only horizontal.\n\nAnother idea: **I10**? No support.\n\nBest move is to extend the three-in-a-row into a four-in-a-row.\n\nSo both F8 and J8 are strong.\n\nBut is either of them threatened?\n\nWait — can Black block both sides?\n\nYes — but if we play **J8**, then we have G8-H8-I8-J8 — four in a row. Then Black must respond to prevent K8.\n\nSimilarly, if we play F8, Black must block E8.\n\nBut here’s a key point: **playing J8 creates an open-ended four if F8 is already favorable** — but actually, both ends are open unless blocked.\n\nBut is there a **better** move?\n\nWait — what about **I7**? Is that part of a diagonal?\n\nG8, H7 — H7 is Black, so no.\n\nOr **G9**? G9 is empty — but G9 would be below G8. G8 is White, H9 is Black — no alignment.\n\nAlternatively, is there a move that creates a **two-way threat**?\n\nConsider **J8**: completes four-in-a-row horizontally on row 8. Strong, but not yet a forced win.\n\nBut crucially — **does White have any open three that can become a four with fork potential?**\n\nCurrently, the horizontal line on row 8 is the only strong structure.\n\nTherefore, extending it to four is best.\n\nNow, between F8 and J8 — both extend the line.\n\nBut **J8** is better because:\n- The action area has more development on the right (I9, H9) — slight momentum toward the right.\n- No immediate Black presence at J8.\n\nBut actually, symmetry suggests it doesn’t matter — but we must pick one.\n\nWait — is **H10** a threat? H10 is below H9 (Black), but H8 is White — so no vertical threat.\n\nBut what if Black plays J9 next? Then H9-I9-J9 — three in a row. But not urgent.\n\nBack to White: best move is to build toward five.\n\nThus, **J8** is a strong candidate.\n\nBut let’s verify availability: J8 is not in the occupied list — yes, free.\n\nSimilarly, F8 is free.\n\nBut here’s a better idea: **is there a move that creates a double threat?**\n\nWhat about **I7**? I7 is above I8.\n\nI8 is White, I7 is empty.\n\nIf White plays I7, then H8-I7? No — diagonal would be H7-I8, but H7 is Black.\n\nDiagonal from G9-H8-I7? G9 is empty, H8 is White, I7 would be White — if G9 is later played, it could form G9-H8-I7 — diagonal up-left to down-right.\n\nBut currently only two stones — weak.\n\nNot better than extending the solid three-in-a-row.\n\nTherefore, best move is to extend horizontally.\n\nNow, why **J8 over F8**?\n\nActually, looking at the pattern:\n- Black has stones at H7, H9, I9 — suggesting they are building toward the southeast.\n- White should control expansion — playing J8 pressures the open right side.\n- Also, I8 is near I9 (Black), so securing J8 prevents Black from playing there and potentially blocking later.\n\nBut strategically, **both F8 and J8 are symmetric**, but since the board has no asymmetry in threats, we can choose either.\n\nHowever, **J8** is slightly better because:\n- It sets up a potential follow-up at J9 or K8, creating dual-direction pressure.\n- F8 is closer to edge, less space beyond.\n\nBut really, minimal difference.\n\nBut wait — **what about blocking future Black play at J8**? If we don\'t take it, Black might not play there anyway.\n\nBut our own attack is stronger.\n\nSo best move: **extend the three-in-a-row to four**.\n\nChoose **J8**.\n\nBut is there a **winning move**?\n\nNo — no five-in-a-row possible in one move.\n\nSo maximize threat.\n\nFinal decision: Play **J8** to form G8-H8-I8-J8 — four in a row, forcing Black to respond.\n\nReason: Extending the existing three-in-a-row horizontally on row 8 to create a strong four-in-a-row threat, maximizing winning chances by creating a nearly unstoppable line.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 8}'}
